    "outcome-final-instance": "refused by the ledger: the instance is final and takes no more calls",
    "outcome-no-request": "refused by the ledger: the instance has no open request {kind}",
    "outcome-early": "refused by the ledger: the request {kind} expires only at {deadline}",
    "outcome-too-many-requests": "refused by the ledger: the instance would have more than 64 open requests",
    "outcome-broken": "the contract's code did not run as compiled code does ({detail}); this is a bug in the compiler: please report it",
    "outcome-no-part": "no single instance to act on: {party} holds none of this test's instances, or more than one",
    "outcome-no-part-step": "no single instance to act on: the step's party is the holder, pledgee, or payee of none of this test's instances that take calls, or of several; name the holder after the action (issuer pay smith1)",
    "outcome-proposed": "kept as a proposal of {action}, which waits for a person's approval; the instance does not change",
    "expect-accepted": "the call is accepted",
    "expect-state": "the call is accepted, and the instance is {state}",
    "expect-refused": "the call is refused",
    "expect-refused-reason": "the call is refused, with the reason {reason}",
    "expect-waits": "the call waits for the consent of {parties}",
    "expect-waits-home": "the call waits for {party} to make it",
    "expect-waits-both": "the call waits for the consent of {parties}, and for {party} to make it",
    "expect-proposed": "the call is kept as a proposal, pending approval",
    "expect-fields": "{what}; then {fields}",
    "outcome-fields": "{what}, but {fields}",
    "field-is": "{field} is {value}",
    "stdlib-starter": "started by {party.object}",
    "stdlib-home": "started by the home ledger, on its own word",
    "stdlib-counterparty": "to a counterparty, who consents",
    "stdlib-states": "{from} → {to}",
    "stdlib-terms": "The standard terms of {action}:",
    "rule-R1": "A field the vocabulary defines has the vocabulary's type: holder, pledgee, and payee a party, due a date, tier a number, customer location a text, cooling period a duration, recipients a list of parties.",
    "rule-R2": "A field given a party holds a party: each instance starts with that party, and actions may change it.",
    "rule-R3": "A role the contract first names as an action's counterparty (after to) is bound to a ledger by that action, with its consent; every other role is bound to a ledger when an instance is created.",
    "rule-R4": "An instance starts in the one state that no state change leads to; if there is none, in the state active.",
    "rule-R5": "The states of an instance are those its state changes name.",
    "rule-R6": "Over N compares N with the action's one amount (its one input of money or a quantity, else the instance's amount); a bare number N is in that amount's unit.",
    "rule-R7": "A state used as a condition means the instance is in that state.",
    "rule-R8": "Changing a field that holds a party takes it from that party, so it needs that party's consent; when that party starts the action, its call is its consent.",
    "rule-R9": "A time rule whose action names a party states that party's duty: it sets when an instance is due and overdue, and nothing is called.",
    "rule-R10": "Notify, and on a service subscribe, upgrade, downgrade, suspend, resume, terminate, allocate, and reclaim, ask an executor to act outside the ledger.",
    "rule-R11": "A service with a recurring price is due at the end of each paid period.",
    "rule-R12": "Customer location is a text each instance holds, given when the instance is created.",
    "rule-R13": "In person asks the party that starts the action to prove presence with a passkey or a hardware key.",
    "rule-R14": "An AI agent may start an action within its delegation, unless the action says AI agent can propose; then the agent only proposes, and a person approves.",
    "rule-R15": "Parts are a list of amounts in the unit of the instance's amount.",
    "rule-R16": "A list holds at most 16 items, unless at most N says otherwise.",
    "rule-R17": "A product or quotient of money, quantities, or rates is rounded to the smallest unit, half up (away from zero).",
    "rule-R18": "A name nothing declares, compared with a text, is that text.",
    "rule-R19": "A built-in action has the standard terms Bo's standard library gives it (cexchain bo stdlib show ACTION): who starts it, whose consent it needs, the states it starts from and leads to, and what it changes. The contract's own clauses come first and add to them; R8 adds no consent to the standard terms.",
    "rule-R20": "The pledgee and the payee, and a resource's holder, used by an action (the standard terms of a built-in action included) but not declared by the contract, are fields of each instance, holding no party until an action sets them.",
    "rule-R21": "An action whose counterparty (after to) is counterparty, a party the contract does not declare, takes its counterparty from each call (the argument counterparty) and needs its consent; so does a built-in action with a counterparty when the contract writes no to.",
    "rule-R22": "A bare 0 compared with, added to, or taken from money, a quantity, a rate, or a duration is zero of it.",
    "rule-R23": "A role that allocate binds is bound again by each allocation, with its consent: a resource goes to one holder at a time, and each allocation names it.",
    "rule-R24": "A ledger acts only on calls, so each time rule is a function that anyone the home delegates may call: a keeper, usually a device of the home calling it on a schedule. The ledger's registration time decides: the rule applies only once its time has come, and once for each such time; an earlier call is refused, and a later one is recorded with how late it came. The rule is the contract's own word: it needs only the consents it states, not those of the parties that start the actions it does.",
    "rule-R25": "A contract that names no UTC offset counts dates and days at UTC+08:00.",
    "rule-R26": "A field named by a document (invoice, credit note, receipt) holds the lines of a bill, each an amount of money. Each time it is set, the charges are computed on it at the time of that call; the instance keeps the bill (its time, the price, each charge that applies, on each line and on the whole bill, and the total to pay), and the call records it as an event.",
    "rule-R27": "Charges are computed in the order written, each on the price (a line's amount, or the whole bill's for a charge computed once per bill) unless according to names what it is computed on: the price and charges written before it. An added charge is its base times its rate, and adds to the total; an included one is taken out of the price: the price times its rate, divided by 100% plus the rates of the included charges that apply. A fixed amount is charged once per bill, or on each line. A charge that does not apply, or has no rate in force, is not charged.",
    "rule-R28": "A charge's rate is the one in force at the bill's time: the rate from the latest date on or before it (from the start of that day, at the contract's UTC offset), else its own rate. A bill keeps the rate it was made with.",
    "rule-R29": "A single value where a list is needed is a list of that one item.",
    "rule-R30": "Issue PARTS to RECIPIENTS makes each part an instance of its own, of the same contract, created by the call: its amount is the part and its holder its recipient, and the instance that issued it ends. A part may be split again, at most eight levels down, and its evidence carries the history of every instance it came from (spec section 15).",
    "rule-R31": "An action that makes its counterparty (after to) the holder, the pledgee, or the payee, as transfer, pledge, and allocate do, needs the counterparty's consent at every call, even when its role was bound to a ledger when the instance was created or by another action: nobody becomes a holder, a pledgee, or a payee without agreeing.",
    "rule-R32": "A duration divided by a duration is the number of whole times the second fits in the first; the rest is dropped (rounded toward zero): 2 days and 20 hours divided by 1 day is 2.",
    "rule-R33": "A request that expires (no answer by its deadline) is treated as one that failed, unless the contract has a rule for its expiry."
  }
}
